steps.body:
Nasdaq's ITCH 5.0 feed sends an Add Order, a new order resting on the book, as 36 bytes with every number big-endian:

| Offset | Bytes | Field |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 0 | 1 | message type, `A` |
| 1 | 2 | stock locate, a number for the symbol |
| 3 | 2 | tracking number |
| 5 | 6 | timestamp, nanoseconds since midnight |
| 11 | 8 | order reference number |
| 19 | 1 | side, `B` or `S` |
| 20 | 4 | shares |
| 24 | 8 | stock symbol, padded with spaces |
| 32 | 4 | price, with 4 implied decimals |

A struct can't simply lie over those bytes. Its fields get padding for alignment, and C++ has no 6-byte integer for the timestamp:

```cpp
#include <cstdint>
#include <iostream>

struct AddOrder {
    char type;
    std::uint16_t locate;
    std::uint16_t tracking;
    std::uint64_t timestamp;  // 6 bytes on the wire, but C++ has no 6-byte integer
    std::uint64_t order;
    char side;
    std::uint32_t shares;
    char stock[8];
    std::uint32_t price;
};

#pragma pack(push, 1)
struct PackedAddOrder {  // the same fields, with no padding
    char type;
    std::uint16_t locate;
    std::uint16_t tracking;
    std::uint64_t timestamp;
    std::uint64_t order;
    char side;
    std::uint32_t shares;
    char stock[8];
    std::uint32_t price;
};
#pragma pack(pop)

int main() {
    std::cout << sizeof(AddOrder) << ' ' << sizeof(PackedAddOrder) << '\n';  // 48 38, and the message is 36
    return 0;
}
```

`#pragma pack(push, 1)` removes the padding, as the advanced lesson *Memory Layout: Alignment, Padding, and Cache Lines* shows, but the fields would still be big-endian. So decoders read field by field. `std::memcpy` copies a field's bytes into a local of the right type, and a **byte swap** reverses them. g++ and clang provide `__builtin_bswap16`, `__builtin_bswap32`, and `__builtin_bswap64`, so `__builtin_bswap32(0x2C010000)` is 0x12C, 300. MSVC calls them `_byteswap_ushort`, `_byteswap_ulong`, and `_byteswap_uint64`. POSIX's `ntohs` and `ntohl`, "network to host", undo the `htons` and `htonl` of *Networking: Sockets, TCP, UDP, and Market Data Feeds* and do nothing on a big-endian machine. C++20 adds `std::endian` to ask which kind of machine you're on, and C++23 adds `std::byteswap`, both in *What C++20 and C++23 Add*.

Don't read a field by pointing a `std::uint32_t*` at `p + 20` and going through it. The address may not be aligned for a `uint32_t`, and it breaks the **strict aliasing rule**: an object may be read only through its own type or through `char`, `unsigned char`, or `std::byte`, so the optimizer assumes a `uint32_t*` never points into a byte buffer. Both are undefined. `memcpy` is the defined way, and it costs nothing. The sandbox's g++ 10.2 at `-O2` compiles the example's `be32` to a load and a swap, and the written-out shifts `std::uint32_t(p[0]) << 24 | ... | p[3]` to the very same two instructions:

```text
_Z4be32PKh:
        mov     eax, DWORD PTR [rdi]
        bswap   eax
        ret
```

On the Mac, clang makes `ldr w8, [x0]` and `rev w0, w8` for both.

example.code:
#include <cstdint>
#include <cstring>
#include <iomanip>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

std::uint16_t be16(const unsigned char* p) {
    std::uint16_t v;
    std::memcpy(&v, p, sizeof v);
    return __builtin_bswap16(v);
}

std::uint32_t be32(const unsigned char* p) {
    std::uint32_t v;
    std::memcpy(&v, p, sizeof v);
    return __builtin_bswap32(v);
}

std::uint64_t be64(const unsigned char* p) {
    std::uint64_t v;
    std::memcpy(&v, p, sizeof v);
    return __builtin_bswap64(v);
}

std::uint64_t be48(const unsigned char* p) {  // the 6-byte timestamp
    return std::uint64_t(be16(p)) << 32 | be32(p + 2);
}

int main() {
    std::vector<unsigned char> msg;
    unsigned int byte = 0;
    while (std::cin >> std::hex >> byte) {
        msg.push_back(static_cast<unsigned char>(byte));
    }
    if (msg.size() != 36 || msg[0] != 'A') {
        std::cout << "not an Add Order\n";
        return 0;
    }
    const unsigned char* p = msg.data();
    std::string stock(msg.begin() + 24, msg.begin() + 32);
    while (!stock.empty() && stock.back() == ' ') {
        stock.pop_back();
    }
    const std::uint32_t price = be32(p + 32);  // 4 implied decimals
    std::cout << "locate " << be16(p + 1) << '\n';
    std::cout << "timestamp " << be48(p + 5) << '\n';
    std::cout << "order " << be64(p + 11) << '\n';
    std::cout << "side " << msg[19] << '\n';
    std::cout << "shares " << be32(p + 20) << '\n';
    std::cout << "stock " << stock << '\n';
    std::cout << "price " << price / 10000 << '.' << std::setfill('0') << std::setw(4) << price % 10000 << '\n';
    return 0;
}

