steps.body:
Feeds pack yes-or-no flags and small codes into single bytes. Say a venue's order flags byte holds the side in bit 0, the time in force in bits 1 and 2, a hidden flag in bit 3, and a venue number in bits 4 to 7. C++ can name such bits with **bit-fields**, members with a width in bits after a colon:

```cpp
struct OrderFlags {
    unsigned side : 1;
    unsigned tif : 2;
    unsigned hidden : 1;
    unsigned venue : 4;
};

std::cout << sizeof(OrderFlags) << '\n';  // 4: the fields share one unsigned
```

With `std::uint8_t` members instead of `unsigned`, the same four fields fit in 1 byte. Their limits:

- A field holds only its width. `f.venue = 17;` keeps 1, and g++ warns: `conversion from 'unsigned int' to 'unsigned char:4' changes value from '17' to '1'`.
- A field has no address, so no pointer or reference can refer to it:

```cpp error
struct OrderFlags {
    unsigned side : 1;
    unsigned venue : 4;
};

OrderFlags f{1, 9};
unsigned* p = &f.venue;
std::cout << *p << '\n';
```

- Which bits each field takes is up to the compiler. g++ and clang on x86-64 and ARM64 fill from bit 0 up, so `OrderFlags{1, 2, 0, 9}` copied out with `memcpy` is the byte 10010101, exactly the layout above. MSVC starts a new storage unit when the member type changes, and big-endian systems fill from the top bit.

So never `memcpy` wire bytes into a bit-field struct. Decode them with the shifts and masks from *Bitwise Operations*, which mean the same on every compiler: `b & 1` is bit 0, `(b >> 1) & 0b11` is bits 1 and 2, and `b >> 4` is the top four. Bit-fields suit your own data in memory, where the layout is nobody else's business.

example.code:
#include <cstdint>
#include <iomanip>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

// An order's flags byte: bit 0 side, bits 1-2 time in force, bit 3 hidden, bits 4-7 venue.
struct OrderFlags {  // bit-fields: compact in memory, but never memcpy'd from the wire
    unsigned side : 1;
    unsigned tif : 2;
    unsigned hidden : 1;
    unsigned venue : 4;
};

OrderFlags decode(std::uint8_t b) {
    OrderFlags f{};
    f.side = b & 1;
    f.tif = (b >> 1) & 0b11;
    f.hidden = (b >> 3) & 1;
    f.venue = b >> 4;
    return f;
}

int main() {
    const std::string tif_names[] = {"DAY", "IOC", "FOK", "GTC"};
    std::cout << "sizeof(OrderFlags): " << sizeof(OrderFlags) << '\n';
    unsigned int byte = 0;
    while (std::cin >> std::hex >> byte) {
        const OrderFlags f = decode(static_cast<std::uint8_t>(byte));
        std::cout << "0x" << std::setfill('0') << std::setw(2) << std::hex << byte << std::dec << ": "
                  << (f.side ? "sell " : "buy ") << tif_names[f.tif] << (f.hidden ? " hidden" : "")
                  << " venue " << f.venue << '\n';
    }
    return 0;
}

