steps.body:
A **union** is a struct whose members all share one place in memory. Its size is its largest member's, and it holds one member at a time, the **active member**: the last one written.

```cpp
union Bits {
    std::uint64_t u;
    double d;
};

Bits b{};      // u, the first member, is active and 0
b.d = 192.75;  // now d is active
std::cout << sizeof(Bits) << ' ' << b.d << '\n';  // 8 192.75
```

A feed that sends a price as the 8 bytes of an IEEE 754 double, big-endian, leaves you holding its bits in a `std::uint64_t` after the swap. Writing them to `b.u` and reading `b.d` looks like a way to get the double, a trick called **type punning**. C allows it. In C++ it's undefined: `d` isn't the active member, so no `double` exists there to read. g++ documents that it works anyway, as an extension, and clang does the same, but the C++ standard doesn't promise it. Where the compiler must catch undefined behavior, while it evaluates a `constexpr` call at compile time, it refuses:

```cpp error
#include <cstdint>
#include <iostream>

union Bits {
    std::uint64_t u;
    double d;
};

constexpr double from_bits(std::uint64_t x) {
    Bits b{};
    b.u = x;
    return b.d;  // reads the member that wasn't written
}

int main() {
    constexpr double px = from_bits(0x4068180000000000);
    std::cout << px << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

g++ says `accessing 'Bits::d' member instead of initialized 'Bits::u' member in constant expression`, and the Mac's clang says `read of member 'd' of union with active member 'u' is not allowed in a constant expression`. A union with a `std::string` member is worse: read it after writing a double, and the price's 8 bytes become the string's pointer.

The defined way is `std::memcpy` into a real `double`, or C++20's `std::bit_cast<double>(bits)` in one call. It costs nothing: g++ 10.2 at `-O2` compiles a `from_bits` written with `memcpy` to the single instruction `movq xmm0, rdi`, exactly what the union version gets, and the Mac's clang makes `fmov d0, x0` for both.

Unions earn their place as a **tagged union**: an enum next to the union records which member is active, and every read checks it first. That's how a decoder holds one of several field types in one value without the heap. `std::variant`, in the advanced lesson *Errors in Fast Code: noexcept, Error Codes, and Results*, is a tagged union the library keeps correct for you.

example.code:
#include <cstdint>
#include <cstring>
#include <iomanip>
#include <iostream>

enum class Kind { Ticks, Price };

struct Value {
    Kind kind;  // the tag: which member of the union is active
    union {
        std::int64_t ticks;
        double price;
    };
};

std::uint64_t be64(const unsigned char* p) {
    std::uint64_t v;
    std::memcpy(&v, p, sizeof v);
    return __builtin_bswap64(v);
}

Value decode(char type, const unsigned char* p) {
    const std::uint64_t bits = be64(p);
    Value v{};
    if (type == 'I') {
        v.kind = Kind::Ticks;
        v.ticks = static_cast<std::int64_t>(bits);
    } else {
        double d;
        std::memcpy(&d, &bits, sizeof d);  // the same 8 bytes, read as a double
        v.kind = Kind::Price;
        v.price = d;
    }
    return v;
}

int main() {
    std::cout << std::fixed << std::setprecision(4);
    char type = 0;
    while (std::cin >> type) {
        unsigned char bytes[8];
        for (unsigned char& b : bytes) {
            unsigned int x = 0;
            std::cin >> std::hex >> x;
            b = static_cast<unsigned char>(x);
        }
        const Value v = decode(type, bytes);
        if (v.kind == Kind::Ticks) {
            std::cout << "ticks " << v.ticks << '\n';
        } else {
            std::cout << "price " << v.price << '\n';
        }
    }
    return 0;
}

